A||||4||acehilton||The debate on crankshafts||||1||
Z||000000||acehilton||06-14-2001||10:56 PM||||OK so here is question; Has anyone ever seen a Mercruiser/GM 181 crank installed in a Duke?  I pulled up Merc's website and did some digging.  On the parts page, Merc lists several (about 12) different L4 engines they have used over the years (starting with the 153 c.i. up to the current 181 c.i.).  The only crank still listed as selling new is the 181 c.i. crank.  According to the exploded (no jokes please) diagram, the crank does not look any beefier than the Fiero's stock crank.  <P>I couldn't find any specs on the cam, but they list the HP as 135 at the propshaft.  I would be willing to bet it is closer to 150 at the crank, but without the cam specs I can't run an accurate dyno simulation.  The possibility of a relieble 150 horse duke, sure makes it seem worth it.<P>You can also purchase a brand new engine (who knows if it will work in the Fiero) for about 4200.00 according to the website.<P>Let me know what you think.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Standard||06-15-2001||03:09 AM||||If you want a stronger crank, you can get one from a 90-91 S-10 2.5 duke which is stronger than the stock ones the Fiero's came with.. the crank isn't the only weak point, the block just isn't built to withstand too much power... and for $4200 you could have a low mile 3.4 (or even a new GM crate one, haven't checked the prices lately) installed and get 180hp and 200lb-ft of torque..||||reg||1
Z||000002||acehilton||06-15-2001||08:33 AM||||I probably wouldn't pay 4 grand + to put any engine in my car (4,6, or 8 cylinders), I am just curious about the possibility of a 3 liter 4 banger.  Besides, I like to work with what I have got, and right now I have a Duke.   <P>Another thing to consider is this; marine engines and components are made to be run , for most of their lives, at or just below the peak power ratings.  Whereas our vehicle engines spend most of their time  at only a fraction of the peak power.  GM has had great success cross pollinating marine and auto technology (the ZR-1 Corvette for starters). <P>My question still stands though, has anyone actually seen (watched the engine put togather and run) one of these marine cranks in a Duke?||||reg||1
Z||000003||Raydar||06-15-2001||09:25 AM||||There is someone on the Forum that is supposed to be in the process of putting the marine crank in the Duke. Sorry, I don't remember who it is, though. He was doing some other extensive mods, too. <BR>It won't work on 87 and later Dukes due to the crank-trigger (DIS) ignition.<BR>You might try doing a search on "merc" and "marine" and see what has been posted. <P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 06-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||erikred||06-15-2001||10:38 PM||||I have not seen this done myself, but I have heard from 3 seperate sources (only one was on this forum) that they have done it. Evedently you need a one piece rear sill adapter and you need to drill and tap the front for a harmonic balancer.<P>You can get 140 3.0 Mercs for at least 1/2 of the $4200 you mentioned, however you would need a SBC adapter plate and you would need to swap on a cross flow head. <P>KRP was selling rebuildable 3.0 engines for $500 a while ago. Talk to Gerry he knows alot about them and is very helpful.<P>BTW if you are only going for 150 HP the stock crank should be fine, unless you constantly flog it.<BR>||||reg||12
